# Tophat (CVE-2026-39862, no CWE recorded; the shape is CWE-78) launches
# Android and Apple devices by building a bash command as one string and
# running it through Process.launchBash, which sets launchPath /bin/bash and
# arguments ["-c", command]. Every dynamic value reaches that string raw:
# a tophat://install?...&arguments=... URL is read by URLReader, whose
# queryItems are binned, subscripted by "arguments", split on commas and
# packed into InstallRecipe.launchArguments; InstallApplicationTask hands
# them to Device.launch, Adb packs them into an --esa extra, and
# ShellCommand.string joins the whole argument list with spaces. The old
# wrappedInQuotationMarks() wraps a value in double quotes, which stops
# neither $(...), backticks, nor ; inside a double-quoted bash word, so a
# crafted URL runs arbitrary commands as the user (and the elevated
# AppleScript path runs them with administrator privileges).
#
# 60f77659425a7888439d1a6795a664f4410a4d45 ("Sanitize launch arguments before
# passing them to bash commands") introduces ShellArgument.safe/unsafe,
# single-quote shellEscaped() for every dynamic argument, and an
# isSafeShellArgument guard in Adb.launch. The pool row's `nocommit` flag is
# wrong: the commit exists and reads in full -- another instance of the
# flag meaning "something is odd about this row", not "the change is
# unavailable".
#
# The three statement lowerings the original review pinned as the chain's
# breaks are in the live frontend: the for-in loop binds its pattern and
# sequence (swift.go:326, swPatternBindings at :776), a bracketed call_suffix
# lowers to an index read of the callee (swIsSubscript, swift.go:861 and the
# call_expression arm at :699), and a switch case pattern's let-bindings
# become assignments of the subject (swSwitch, swift.go:385, swLetBindings at
# :802). Every distilled case below therefore reports through the system()
# stand-in, and the suite holds them as regression coverage for those
# lowerings. Cases two and three read the opened url directly rather than
# through URLComponents on purpose: `components.queryItems` carries its own
# code.HttpInput label (swift's `.query` binding), which is active wherever
# no profile prunes it and would carry those two cases on their own,
# construct or no construct.
#
# Re-measured at the current state, three --cache off runs per revision of
# the real checkout: exactly one finding on the vulnerable parent and the
# same one on the fixed revision -- VYQL-DOS-040, a ReachableAssertionReview
# on EventMonitor.swift:24 that predates and survives the change -- and the
# review-flag view holds that same single flag on both sides. The source and
# the chain are both live on the real shape: the application(_:open:)
# seeding labels TophatApp.swift:201, and on a distilled probe of the real
# sink the opened URL's query value flows through read, the case-pattern
# binding and the joined string into Process.launchBash#param#command and on
# to the ["-c", command] collection element. The miss is the sink alone.
#
# The sink is not bindable today. Foundation's Process receives its command
# through property assignments -- launchPath and arguments -- whose targets
# leave no node in the graph (only the assigned values do, as
# Const/Seq/CollectionElement), so no binding can key on them; the two
# Foundation-visible calls carry no arguments at all (Process() constructs,
# launch() spawns), so the generated commandExecution20/21 args[0] sinks
# cannot land. The labels those bindings do emit on this tree sit on
# untainted first arguments -- the serial/udid/application args of the app's
# own Adb.launch, DeviceCtl.launch and SimCtl.launch -- and Swift has no
# per-function presence facts (swift.go emits one file-wide
# analysis.module.context and never populates FuncDef.ContextTokens), so the
# only call-argument-visible bash site is the app's own launchBash helper,
# which fails the documentation test: a binding naming it fires on one
# application in the world. The run(command:) code.SqlExecution mislabel
# (generated sqlExecution19, callee.method == "run") does fire VYQL-INJ-001
# on the real shape, but it reports a SQL injection that is not one and
# fires on the patched form identically, so it is not detection.
#
# Case 7 pins the miss with the real sink shape and asserts reject, because
# no command-injection judgement can reach a property-configured Foundation
# process launch under the current routes. When a swift sink route can see
# one -- an assign-position binding route, or per-function presence facts --
# case 7 must flip to expect while the stand-in cases keep reporting and the
# constant-array reject stays silent. A reject for the patched direction
# additionally waits on a generic swift shell-escape control: the real
# patch's isSafeShellArgument guard and single-quote wrapping are
# application-internal, and case 6 records that they leave the stand-in sink
# reporting.

test "tophat: url-scheme parameter read whole reaches the shell through the shipped sink"
  lang swift
  file AppDelegate.swift
  expect VYQL-INJ-002
  code
  ```
  import Foundation

  final class AppDelegate: NSObject, NSApplicationDelegate {
  	func application(_ application: NSApplication, open urls: [URL]) {
  		system("adb shell " + urls.description)
  	}
  }
  ```

test "tophat: a constant array through the same loop and sink reports nothing"
  lang swift
  file Constant.swift
  reject VYQL-INJ-002
  code
  ```
  import Foundation

  final class AppDelegate: NSObject, NSApplicationDelegate {
  	func application(_ application: NSApplication, open urls: [URL]) {
  		for url in [URL(filePath: "/usr/bin/open")] {
  			system("adb shell " + url.description)
  		}
  	}
  }
  ```

